  "notes": "Def Jam's jazz-trained neo-soul soprano. `confidence` is **low** because the research rates the entry medium-low — the label, the era and the jazz-training position are sourced, but \"parametric detail is inference and marked\". The one distinguishing claim is rhythmic and it is what the swing setting encodes: \"a swung, brushed or lightly-programmed kit with an audible shuffle — she is the section's most jazz-rhythm-facing radio artist; snare on 2&4 with ghost strokes, ride or brushes rather than 16th hats, upright-bass-friendly space\". So `swing.amount` is pushed to 0.68, the highest in this section and well above the parent's 0.63, the hat is a non-continuous 8th at a fill density of 0.16 with the ride carrying the time instead, and the percussion lanes are ride-led. Harmony is \"ii–V–I turnarounds, maj7/m7 voicings, and swing-era borrowings; minor keys for the mid-tempos, flat majors for the ballads\", which is the two turnaround families in both modes. Melody is \"bright soprano with a pronounced vibrato and jazz-phrased rhythmic displacement (she sings behind the beat and catches up), moderate note-density, and scat-syllable tags\" — the high register, `endVariation` for the tags, and a question-answer structure. Bass is \"upright or round electric walking in quarters/8ths with chromatic approach\". ⚠ Every drum, melody and bass number here is INFERRED, including `swing.amount` — \"audible shuffle\" is a direction, and 0.68 is this author's reading of it. ⚠ The sub-808 \"only on the crossover cuts\" is not authored, since a `bass808` block would silence the walking bass this entry is built on.",
